<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Osijek</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Osijek is the largest city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a> region in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drava">Drava</a> river near its confluence with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. Successor to the Roman town of Mursa, it was held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> from 1526 to 1687, after which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> built the star-shaped fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tvrda">Tvrđa</a>. It became the administrative and economic centre of Slavonia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/osijek.jpg" alt="Osijek" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nick Savchenko from Kiev, Ukraine · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Osijek_(29263092303).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Osijek stands on the southern bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drava">Drava</a>, 25 kilometres upstream from its confluence with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, at an elevation of 94 metres, and 16 kilometres west of the Croatian border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. Its name comes from its location on elevated ground, which gave protection from floods in the surrounding swamps, and derives from the Croatian word oseka, meaning ebb tide. It is known as Eszék in Hungarian, Esseg in German and Ösek in Turkish.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Human habitation dates to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>. The first known inhabitants were the Illyrian Andizetes, and in the second half of the 4th century BCE the Celtic Scordisci captured the settlement. After the Roman conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>, Mursa was garrisoned by the Seventh Legion, which built a castrum and a bridge across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drava">Drava</a>; Hadrian is thought to have founded it anew and raised it to a colony in 133 CE. Several decisive battles were fought near it, and Slavic tribes repopulated the region in the 6th and 7th centuries. The name Osijek is first recorded in 1196. From 1353 to 1472 the town was a possession of the Kórógyi family.</p><p>An Ottoman army sacked and nearly destroyed the town on 8 August 1526, just before the Battle of Mohács, and a permanent garrison was established in 1529. In 1566 the Ottoman ruler Suleiman commissioned an eight-kilometre wooden pontoon bridge, regarded in its time as one of the wonders of the world. Osijek became a significant commercial centre for East-West trade. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> forces captured it on 29 September 1687, ending over 150 years of Ottoman rule.</p><p>Between 1712 and 1715 the Austrians built the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tvrda">Tvrđa</a>, designed by Maximilian Gosseau de Henef, with five bastions. The Upper Town was founded in 1692 and the Lower Town in 1698, and in 1786 the three districts were united in one city. German immigrants settled there, giving rise to a local dialect, Essekerisch. Osijek became a Free Royal City in 1809 and was then the largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. The Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a> was founded in 1877. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the German-speaking population was expelled, and the University of Osijek was established in 1975.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Osijek is the largest city and the economic and cultural hub of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a>, the seat of Osijek-Baranja County and the fourth-largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, with 96,313 inhabitants at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. During the Croatian War of Independence it was heavily shelled, from August 1991 to June 1992 alone about 800 people were killed, and the Co-cathedral of St. Peter and St. Paul was badly damaged. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> citadel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tvrda">Tvrđa</a>, the Neo-Gothic co-cathedral, whose tower rises to 90 metres, and a tram network running since 1884 are among its landmarks.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Osijek” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Osijek's</b> fortress, Tvrđa, was rebuilt in the 1710s and 1720s, and the bishopric of Đakovo revived Catholic life, while Orthodox Serbs were organised around Sremski Karlovci.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-slavonia">Kingdom of Slavonia</a></li></ul></section>
